{"/api/discogs/artist/1158079/profile":{"cached":true,"profile":{"id":"1158079","image":"https://i.discogs.com/TQaSrGyD4NPpD3BPK3JK7grLP3s4ZSaV_fZHmI6m9qk/rs:fit/g:sm/q:90/h:446/w:480/czM6Ly9kaXNjb2dz/LWRhdGFiYXNlLWlt/YWdlcy9BLTExNTgw/NzktMTYyMDkxNzc1/MC02ODk3LmpwZWc.jpeg","imageThumb":"https://i.discogs.com/2N6KvWmuFcB3sF1YHBK8a87g74GHVMCptHoTtr08EpE/rs:fit/g:sm/q:40/h:150/w:150/czM6Ly9kaXNjb2dz/LWRhdGFiYXNlLWlt/YWdlcy9BLTExNTgw/NzktMTYyMDkxNzc1/MC02ODk3LmpwZWc.jpeg","profile":"Sequel to [a1009771] after a line-up change in 1970, during which year they also switched to the UK's [l142472] label, for whom they recorded two albums and two 45's with a more mainstream sound, taking in influences such as Traffic and The Band. Both albums were comprised entirely of [a=David Lewis (5)] compositions (except for one instrumental track on The World's End, which was released when they had just changed their name). David Lewis was a multi-instrumentalist and an acclaimed songwriter who also recorded a privately-pressed album in 1970. He also made further solo albums. [a=Jack McCulloch] had previously played with [a=One In A Million], [a=Thunderclap Newman] and with [a=Dave Struthers] had been with short-lived group McCullochs, Struthers And Patterson (the other members being [a=Jimmy McCulloch] and Robbie Patterson). After Andwella he went on to a group called White Line with his brother Jimmy. [a=Dave McDougall (2)] went on to work with [a=John \"Speedy\" Keen] (ex-Thunderclap Newman). Andwella is also said to be backing band on [m1960771] (1970), an album produced by Lewis.","provider":"discogs","ref":"discogs:artist:1158079","relations":{"aliases":null,"groups":null,"members":[{"active":true,"id":902869,"name":"Jack McCulloch"},{"active":false,"id":1101931,"name":"Gordon Barton"},{"active":false,"id":1158076,"name":"Nigel Smith (4)"},{"active":true,"id":1158077,"name":"Dave McDougall (2)"},{"active":true,"id":1158078,"name":"David Lewis (5)"},{"active":true,"id":1158086,"nam